From: Buildroot <buildroot@buildroot.org>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] kfio_config.sh: build capability probes with the kernel toolchain

kfio_config.sh builds its kernel capability probes with

    env -i PATH="${PATH}" make -C "$test_dir"

so the probes are always compiled by the *host* compiler, for the host
architecture, no matter how the kernel itself was built.  The probes are
compiled with -Werror (since "pull in add_disk kfio_config fix from
vsl-#164" changed the obsolete EXTRA_CFLAGS assignment to ccflags-y+=
and thereby made the flag effective), so any diagnostic the host
compiler emits while parsing the kernel headers turns every probe into a
failure.

That is exactly what happens on Buildroot: the kernel is configured and
built with the GCC 16 cross toolchain, so CONFIG_CC_HAS_COUNTED_BY and
CONFIG_CC_HAS_COUNTED_BY_PTR are enabled and __counted_by() /
__counted_by_ptr() expand to the real attributes.  The probes, however,
are compiled with the host compiler (GCC 14 in the CI image), which does
not implement those attributes:

    include/linux/fs.h:1302:9: error: 'counted_by' attribute directive
    ignored [-Werror=attributes]

All KFIOC_X_* probes therefore return 0, the driver selects the legacy
(pre-5.6) kernel APIs, and the module build then fails on the first API
that really is mandatory, e.g.:

    kfile.c:226:47: error: passing argument 4 of 'proc_create_data' from
    incompatible pointer type [-Wincompatible-pointer-types]
    note: expected 'const struct proc_ops *' but argument is of type
    'struct file_operations *'

Pass ARCH and CROSS_COMPILE through to the probe builds when the caller
provides them, so the probes are built the same way as the kernel and
the driver itself: for the target architecture with the cross compiler.
Standalone invocations without ARCH/CROSS_COMPILE keep the old
behaviour.

Signed-off-by: Buildroot <buildroot@buildroot.org>
---
 root/usr/src/iomemory-vsl4-4.3.7/kfio_config.sh | 15 +++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/root/usr/src/iomemory-vsl4-4.3.7/kfio_config.sh b/root/usr/src/iomemory-vsl4-4.3.7/kfio_config.sh
--- a/root/usr/src/iomemory-vsl4-4.3.7/kfio_config.sh
+++ b/root/usr/src/iomemory-vsl4-4.3.7/kfio_config.sh
@@ -888,10 +888,21 @@ MODULE_LICENSE(\"GPL\");
     fi
     echo "$license" >> "$test_dir/kfioc_test.c"
 
+    # Build the probes with the same architecture and cross compiler that
+    # the kernel was built with, when the caller provides them.  Otherwise
+    # the probes silently use the host compiler, which may not support
+    # attributes the kernel was configured for (e.g. __counted_by(), which
+    # is enabled based on the compiler that configured the kernel).  The
+    # probes are built with -Werror, so such a mismatch makes every test
+    # fail and the driver then falls back to ancient kernel APIs.
+    local probe_env="PATH=${PATH}"
+    [ -n "$ARCH" ] && probe_env="$probe_env ARCH=$ARCH"
+    [ -n "$CROSS_COMPILE" ] && probe_env="$probe_env CROSS_COMPILE=$CROSS_COMPILE"
+
     if [ 1 -eq "$VERBOSE" ]; then
-        env -i PATH="${PATH}" make -C "$test_dir" V=1 2>&1 | tee "$test_dir/kfioc_test.log" || result=$?
+        env -i $probe_env make -C "$test_dir" V=1 2>&1 | tee "$test_dir/kfioc_test.log" || result=$?
     else
-        env -i PATH="${PATH}" make -C "$test_dir" V=1 >"$test_dir/kfioc_test.log" 2>&1 || result=$?
+        env -i $probe_env make -C "$test_dir" V=1 >"$test_dir/kfioc_test.log" 2>&1 || result=$?
     fi
 
     # Save the exit status
-- 
2.55.0
